<section class="lesson-card" id="binomial">
<h2>2. Binomial: repeated yes-or-no trials</h2>
<p>The Binomial distribution applies when you have a <strong>fixed number of independent trials</strong>, each trial has only two outcomes, and the probability of the selected outcome is constant.</p>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="concept-card">
<h3>Pipe inspection example</h3>
<p>You inspect 20 pipes. Each pipe either passes or fails.</p>
<ul>
<li>Trial: one pipe</li>
<li>Selected outcome: fail</li>
<li>Number of trials: n = 20</li>
<li>Probability of failure: p</li>
<li>Random variable: number of failed pipes</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="concept-card">
<h3>Why this is not Poisson</h3>
<p>Each pipe contributes at most one selected outcome: fail or not fail. The maximum possible count is therefore 20.</p>
<p>Poisson is different: one pipe or one inspection interval may contain several defects.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div aria-label="Binomial probability formula" class="formula">
    P(X=x)=
    <span class="fraction"><span>n!</span><span>x!(n-x)!</span></span>
    p<sup>x</sup>(1-p)<sup>n-x</sup>
</div>
<p>The expected number of selected outcomes is <strong>np</strong>, and the variance is <strong>np(1-p)</strong>.</p>
<h3>Live Binomial simulator</h3>
<div class="controls">
<div class="control">
<label for="binN">Number of trials n: <span class="value-badge" id="binNValue">20</span></label>
<input id="binN" max="100" min="5" step="1" type="range" value="20"/>
</div>
<div class="control">
<label for="binP">Probability p: <span class="value-badge" id="binPValue">0.10</span></label>
<input id="binP" max="0.80" min="0.01" step="0.01" type="range" value="0.10"/>
</div>
<div class="control">
<label for="binRepeats">Repeated samples: <span class="value-badge" id="binRepeatsValue">500</span></label>
<input id="binRepeats" max="3000" min="100" step="100" type="range" value="500"/>
</div>
</div>
<div class="button-row">
<button class="btn" id="runBinomialBtn" type="button">Run Binomial simulation</button>
<button class="btn secondary" id="resetBinomialBtn" type="button">Reset Binomial example</button>
</div>
<canvas aria-describedby="binomialDescription" aria-label="Histogram of simulated Binomial outcomes" height="360" id="binomialCanvas" role="img" width="720"></canvas>
<p class="chart-caption" id="binomialDescription">Simulation histogram showing the frequency of each selected-outcome count.</p>
<div class="metric-row">
<div class="metric"><small>Theoretical mean np</small><strong id="binTheoMean">–</strong></div>
<div class="metric"><small>Observed mean</small><strong id="binObsMean">–</strong></div>
<div class="metric"><small>Maximum possible count</small><strong id="binMax">–</strong></div>
<div class="metric"><small>Observed range</small><strong id="binRange">–</strong></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: Rare-event approximation explorer -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="rare-event-approximation">
<h2>4. When Poisson can approximate Binomial</h2>
<p class="section-intro">A Binomial process remains Binomial, but when <strong>n is large</strong> and <strong>p is small</strong>, a Poisson distribution with <strong>μ = np</strong> can closely approximate its probabilities. Use this as a computational approximation—not as a change in the data-generating mechanism.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Practical screening rule:</strong> the approximation is often reasonable when p ≤ 0.05 and np is not large. Always compare against the exact Binomial result when software is available.</div>
<div class="interactive-shell">
<div class="compact-controls">
<div class="control"><label for="approxN">Trials n: <span class="value-badge" id="approxNValue">100</span></label><input id="approxN" max="500" min="20" step="10" type="range" value="100"/></div>
<div class="control"><label for="approxP">Probability p: <span class="value-badge" id="approxPValue">0.020</span></label><input id="approxP" max="0.200" min="0.002" step="0.002" type="range" value="0.020"/></div>
<div class="control"><label for="approxX">Selected count x: <span class="value-badge" id="approxXValue">2</span></label><input id="approxX" max="20" min="0" step="1" type="range" value="2"/></div>
<div class="control"><label>Matching Poisson mean</label><div class="metric"><small>μ = np</small><strong id="approxMu">2.000</strong></div></div>
</div>
<div class="chart-wrap">
<canvas aria-describedby="approxDescription" aria-label="Comparison of exact Binomial and Poisson-approximation probabilities" height="360" id="approxCanvas" role="img" width="720"></canvas>
<p class="chart-caption" id="approxDescription">Blue bars are exact Binomial probabilities; amber bars are Poisson probabilities with μ = np.</p>
</div>
<div class="kpi-strip">
<div class="kpi"><span>Exact Binomial P(X=x)</span><strong id="approxBinProb">—</strong></div>
<div class="kpi"><span>Poisson approximation</span><strong id="approxPoiProb">—</strong></div>
<div class="kpi"><span>Difference</span><strong id="approxDifference">—</strong></div>
</div>
<p aria-live="polite" class="status-line" id="approxMessage"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="live-lab">
<h2>3. Live Poisson–Exponential process laboratory</h2>
<p>Adjust the event rate and inspection length. The same generated event locations are then viewed as both <strong>counts</strong> and <strong>gaps</strong>.</p>
<div class="controls">
<div class="control">
<label for="rateSlider">Average rate λ: <span class="value-badge" id="rateValue">2.0</span> events per 100 m</label>
<input id="rateSlider" max="8" min="0.5" step="0.5" type="range" value="2"/>
</div>
<div class="control">
<label for="lengthSlider">Total simulated length: <span class="value-badge" id="lengthValue">1000</span> m</label>
<input id="lengthSlider" max="3000" min="300" step="100" type="range" value="1000"/>
</div>
<div class="control">
<label for="windowSlider">Counting window: <span class="value-badge" id="windowValue">100</span> m</label>
<input id="windowSlider" max="300" min="50" step="50" type="range" value="100"/>
</div>
</div>
<div class="button-row">
<button class="btn" id="resimulateBtn" type="button">Generate a new random process</button>
<button class="btn secondary" id="resetLabBtn" type="button">Reset example</button>
</div>
<div class="callout warn" hidden="" id="labError"></div>
<div class="sim-grid">
<div class="panel">
<h3>Poisson view: events per window</h3>
<canvas aria-describedby="countDescription" aria-label="Bar chart of event counts in fixed windows" height="360" id="countCanvas" role="img" width="720"></canvas>
<p class="small" id="countDescription">Each bar answers: “How many events occurred in this fixed window?”</p>
</div>
<div class="panel">
<h3>Exponential view: distance between events</h3>
<canvas aria-describedby="gapDescription" aria-label="Timeline showing event locations and gaps" height="360" id="gapCanvas" role="img" width="720"></canvas>
<p class="small" id="gapDescription">Each space answers: “How far did we travel before the next event?”</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="metric-row">
<div class="metric"><small>Observed events</small><strong id="mEvents">–</strong></div>
<div class="metric"><small>Observed rate / 100 m</small><strong id="mRate">–</strong></div>
<div class="metric"><small>Mean count/window</small><strong id="mCount">–</strong></div>
<div class="metric"><small>Mean gap</small><strong id="mGap">–</strong></div>
</div>
<div class="bridge">
<div class="callout"><strong>Poisson summary</strong><br/>Count all events inside equal windows.</div>
<div class="bridge-arrow">⇄</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Exponential summary</strong><br/>Measure the distance between each consecutive pair.</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="formulas">
<h2>4. The formulas—and what they actually mean</h2>
<h3>Binomial probability</h3>
<div aria-label="Binomial probability formula" class="formula">
P(X=x)=
<span class="fraction"><span>n!</span><span>x!(n-x)!</span></span>
p<sup>x</sup>(1-p)<sup>n-x</sup>
</div>
<p><strong>n</strong> is the fixed number of trials, <strong>x</strong> is the number of selected outcomes, and <strong>p</strong> is the probability of that outcome on each trial.</p>
<h3>Poisson probability</h3>
<div aria-label="Poisson probability formula" class="formula">
        P(X = x) = <span class="fraction"><span>e<sup>−μ</sup> μ<sup>x</sup></span><span>x!</span></span>
</div>
<p><strong>X</strong> is the event count, <strong>x</strong> is a particular whole-number count, and <strong>μ</strong> is the expected number of events in the selected window.</p>
<div class="formula">μ = λt</div>
<p>If λ is 2 defects per 100 m and the window is 300 m, then μ = 2 × 3 = 6 expected defects.</p>
<h3>Exponential waiting probability</h3>
<div aria-label="Exponential survival formula" class="formula">P(T &gt; t) = e<sup>−λt</sup></div>
<p><strong>T</strong> is the waiting time or distance. This equation gives the probability of waiting <em>longer than</em> t without seeing the next event.</p>
<div class="formula">E(T) = <span class="fraction"><span>1</span><span>λ</span></span></div>
<p>The average gap is the reciprocal of the rate. At 2 defects per 100 m, the mean gap is 100 ÷ 2 = 50 m.</p>
<h3>The powerful bridge</h3>
<div class="formula">P(T &gt; t) = P[N(t)=0] = e<sup>−λt</sup></div>
<p>This is the connection in one line: <strong>waiting longer than t for the next event means exactly zero events occurred during the next t units.</strong></p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Required assumptions:</strong> events occur independently, the average rate is approximately constant, two events are not occurring at exactly the same instant/location in the idealized model, and the process is observed consistently.</div>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: Memoryless property explorer -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="memoryless">
<h2>6. Exponential memorylessness—what it does and does not mean</h2>
<p>The Exponential distribution is <strong>memoryless</strong>: under a constant event rate, time already waited does not change the probability of waiting an additional amount. Mathematically, P(T &gt; s + t | T &gt; s) = P(T &gt; t).</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Important limitation:</strong> memorylessness is a property of the model, not a claim that real equipment never ages. Wear-out, maintenance, seasonality, or changing loads can make the hazard change with time.</div>
<div class="interactive-shell">
<div class="controls">
<div class="control"><label for="memoryRate">Rate λ: <span class="value-badge" id="memoryRateValue">0.020</span> per unit</label><input id="memoryRate" max="0.100" min="0.005" step="0.005" type="range" value="0.020"/></div>
<div class="control"><label for="memoryElapsed">Already waited s: <span class="value-badge" id="memoryElapsedValue">40</span></label><input id="memoryElapsed" max="150" min="0" step="5" type="range" value="40"/></div>
<div class="control"><label for="memoryExtra">Additional wait t: <span class="value-badge" id="memoryExtraValue">30</span></label><input id="memoryExtra" max="150" min="5" step="5" type="range" value="30"/></div>
</div>
<div class="memory-bars">
<div class="memory-bar"><span>P(T &gt; s + t)</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill secondary" id="memoryTotalBar"></div></div><strong id="memoryTotalValue">—</strong></div>
<div class="memory-bar"><span>P(T &gt; s + t | T &gt; s)</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="memoryConditionalBar"></div></div><strong id="memoryConditionalValue">—</strong></div>
<div class="memory-bar"><span>P(T &gt; t), starting fresh</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" id="memoryFreshBar"></div></div><strong id="memoryFreshValue">—</strong></div>
</div>
<p aria-live="polite" class="status-line good" id="memoryMessage"></p>
</div>
</section>
